I’m using the repository’s `crimes-codebase-risk` workflow because this is a finding-driven refactor review. I’ll inspect the actual `crimes context` output and `Card.tsx`, then map a concrete proposal to all three finding types without changing files.

The fixture confirms the three issues are concentrated in one overloaded clickable container: it owns interaction, layout, and visual styling. The global `crimes` binary isn’t installed here, so I’m invoking the repository’s built CLI directly to retrieve the required context report.

Refactor `Card` into three concerns: a semantic container, an explicit action, and token-backed responsive styling. This avoids turning arbitrary `children` into button content.

| Finding type | Proposed change |
|---|---|
| `accessible_interaction_risk` | Remove `onClick` from the outer `<div>`. Use an `<article>` plus a real `<button type="button">` with a visible label. If activation navigates, use `<a href>` instead. This supplies native focus, keyboard activation, and screen-reader semantics. |
| `design_token_escape` | Move inline styles into a CSS module or utility classes backed by the design system. Reuse `brand`, `surface`, and `ink`; add named border, muted-text, shadow, spacing, and focus-ring tokens rather than relocating raw literals. |
| `responsive_fragility` | Remove fixed `800`, `720`, `600`, and `240` dimensions. Make the card `inline-size: 100%` with a tokenized maximum, allow height to follow content, and use a one-column grid that becomes two columns through a named container breakpoint. Use `rem`/`clamp()` typography. |

A suitable component shape would be:

```tsx
import styles from "./Card.module.css";

type CardProps = {
  children: React.ReactNode;
  onActivate: () => void;
  actionLabel: string;
};

export function Card({
  children,
  onActivate,
  actionLabel,
}: CardProps) {
  return (
    <article className={styles.card}>
      <div className={styles.layout}>
        <div className={styles.content}>{children}</div>
        <aside className={styles.sidebar}>sidebar</aside>
      </div>

      <button
        className={styles.action}
        type="button"
        onClick={onActivate}
      >
        {actionLabel}
      </button>
    </article>
  );
}
```

The corresponding styles should follow this structure:

```css
.card {
  container: card / inline-size;
  inline-size: 100%;
  max-inline-size: var(--layout-card-max);
  padding: var(--space-6);
  color: var(--color-ink);
  background: var(--color-surface);
  border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  box-shadow: var(--shadow-card);
}

.layout {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
  gap: var(--space-6);
}

.content {
  min-inline-size: 0;
  font-size: clamp(var(--text-base), 2cqi, var(--text-lg));
}

.sidebar {
  color: var(--color-text-muted);
  font-size: var(--text-sm);
}

.action:focus-visible {
  outline: var(--focus-ring-width) solid var(--color-brand);
  outline-offset: var(--focus-ring-offset);
}

@container card (min-width: 48rem) {
  .layout {
    grid-template-columns:
      minmax(0, 1fr)
      var(--layout-sidebar-width);
  }
}
```

The `48rem` breakpoint should ideally come from the project’s named build-time container scale. Since `crimes context` found no likely tests, I’d add coverage for keyboard activation, the accessible action name, and narrow/wide container layouts before considering the refactor complete. No files were changed.
